Q: How to use tailwind css classes inside WP page content? A: <p>Never use the Play CDN for production. Relying on a server isn't ideal, but there's more to it: Tailwind CSS is a dev tool that generates compiled CSS by analyzing source files; the Play CDN runs this process on every page load, analyzing and generating the same CSS each time. That's bad.</p>
<p>Instead, set up a Tailwind CLI and add its generated <code>output.css</code> to the WordPress <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. You should never load <code>input.css</code> in WordPress.</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://tailwindcss.com/docs/installation/tailwind-cli" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://tailwindcss.com/docs/installation/tailwind-cli</a></li>
<li><a href="https://developer.wordpress.org/themes/core-concepts/including-assets" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.wordpress.org/themes/core-concepts/including-assets</a></li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;!-- Tailwind compiled CSS --&gt;
  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;/path/to/src/output.css&quot;&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>However, when I add Tailwind classes inside WP page content (from the block editor), the style doesn't work.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There are two types of &quot;not working&quot; you might be referring to. One occurs during development - simply use <code>watch</code> mode so that <code>output.css</code> is regenerated whenever you modify any file.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>npx @tailwindcss/cli -i ./src/input.css -o ./src/output.css --watch
</code></pre>
<p>The other issue involves bypassing the browser's caching mechanism. During development, this can be activated via <code>DevTools -&gt; Network -&gt; Disable cache</code>, and it only applies when DevTools is open.</p>
<p>However, this does not solve the production problem. It's important to know that bundlers like Webpack or Vite handle this automatically: whenever a new frontend build is released, the CSS and JS files are assigned new hashes, so their URLs change for the browser. Since the URL changes, the file isn't loaded from cache but fetched again from the server.</p>
<p>Because you're not using a bundler, you need another form of automation, or you have to do this manually using query parameters like <code>?</code> to force the browser to fetch the updated file.</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;!-- Tailwind compiled CSS --&gt;
  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;/path/to/src/output.css?v=2026-03-04-233030&quot;&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: <em>The paths are just examples; of course, replace them with the ones that match your project.</em></p>

Q: How to access the output parameter in node mssql? A: <p>After analysing 1 hour, I found this. Hope it will be useful to someone</p>
<p>Instead of this <strong>request.parameters.SQLReturn</strong> you can use  <strong>recordsets.output.SQLReturn</strong></p>
<pre><code>request.output(&quot;SQLReturn&quot;);

request.execute('sample', function (err, recordsets, returnValue, results) {    
    res.json({ code: 1, message: recordsets.output.SQLReturn });
}
</code></pre>
